Z||000000||Slapnutz2000||08-25-2001||02:01 PM||||Is this normal?  I have a Pioneer DEH-45DH CD player.<P>I lose all memory settings and I'm sick of having to reset the bass and radio station settings every time I take the faceplate out.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||08-25-2001||03:07 PM||||How do you remember where you parked?<P>LOL<P>Sorry, I couldn't help it!<BR>Is you constant 12 volt feed hooked up to the correct wire on the head unit?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ambush||08-25-2001||03:08 PM||||No that is not normal. I would go back to the place that installed it and have them reinstall, if you did it yourself make sure that the radio has a power connection somewhere that is always on. Some face plates have a small watch battery in them to retain memory. Check to see if your model has it. um um that's all I can think of now. Let me know when you fix it, what the problem was.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||08-25-2001||03:10 PM||||Your constant power lead is not wired in properly. Sounds like the deck is running off the switched power from the ignition key. I'd fix that, or you'll blow a fuse/fusible link. It could be that you are running the power/switched power backwards.<P>Alternatively, the deck is cacked. I'd assume your wiring is flocked, though, first.<P>Edit:<P>With that deck, there is no internal power supply. The deck uses the constant power to keep the memory. The faceplate holds no data. You CAN use faceplates from other models (up or down) on that deck. All the functions are internal to the head unit. Again, my thought would be wiring problem.<p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 08-25-2001).]||||reg||1
